## Every weight, every token

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 small is *dense*: all 124 million of its weights work on every token, so a bigger dense
model is slower for every token. A <GlossaryPopover term="mixture-of-experts">mixture of experts</GlossaryPopover>
gets around this. It splits the MLP in each block into several smaller ones, the **experts**, and
adds a small **router** that sends each token to only the best few.<Cite id="shazeer2017" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

GPT-2 small is *dense*: every one of its 124 million weights takes part in predicting every token.
Making a dense model bigger makes every token more expensive to process. A <GlossaryPopover term="mixture-of-experts">mixture of experts</GlossaryPopover>
gets around this. Take the MLP inside each block (<ChapterRef to="the-transformer-block" />) and split it into several smaller MLPs,
the **experts**. In front of them goes a small **router**: for each token, it scores every expert
and sends the token only to the best few.<Cite id="shazeer2017" />

</At>

Here is the router at work on "The cat sat on the", choosing 2 of 8 experts for each token. Pick a
token, and change how many experts each token gets:

<ExpertRouter />

<At levels="simple">

Each token's result blends the outputs of its chosen experts. The other experts don't run for
that token at all.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Each token's output is a blend of its chosen experts' outputs, weighted by the router's scores. The
other experts don't run at all for that token, so their weights cost nothing. Different tokens go to
different experts, so over a whole text every expert gets used.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The router, written down

For a token's vector $x$, the router multiplies by a weight matrix $W_g$ to get one score per
expert, keeps the top $k$, and turns those into weights with a softmax. The output is the weighted
sum of the chosen experts' outputs:

$$y = \sum_{i \in \text{top } k} g_i(x)\, E_i(x), \qquad g(x) = \operatorname{softmax}\big(\text{top}_k(x W_g)\big)$$

That's Mixtral's layer, with $k = 2$.<Cite id="jiang2024" /> The widget takes a softmax over all
eight scores and then rescales the top $k$ to add up to 1, which gives the same weights.

Only the MLPs are split; attention and the embeddings are shared by every token. That's why Mixtral
uses 13 billion weights per token, not a quarter of 47. If each expert has $E$ weights and $S$ are
shared, then $S + 8E = 47$ billion and $S + 2E = 13$ billion, so each expert holds about 5.7
billion and about 1.7 billion are shared.

Training needs a push to spread the work. A router that favors a few experts trains them more,
which makes it favor them even more, so Shazeer and his colleagues added a loss term that penalizes
uneven use.<Cite id="shazeer2017" /> DeepSeek-V3 balances its experts without an extra loss
term.<Cite id="deepseek2024" />

</At>

## Big, but cheap to run

<At levels="simple">

Many large open models work this way. Mixtral 8x7B has 47 billion weights but uses only 13
billion for each token.<Cite id="jiang2024" /> DeepSeek-V3 has 671 billion and uses 37 billion.<Cite id="deepseek2024" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

This is how many large open models are built. Mixtral 8x7B has 8 experts in each block and sends
each token to 2 of them: it has 47 billion weights in total, but each token uses only 13 billion,
about a quarter.<Cite id="jiang2024" /> DeepSeek-V3 goes further: 671 billion weights in total, 37
billion per token, about 6%.<Cite id="deepseek2024" />

</At>

<At levels="simple">

You might imagine one expert for grammar and one for cooking, but trained experts don't split the
work that neatly. In Mixtral, they followed things like code syntax more than topics.<Cite id="jiang2024" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

It is tempting to imagine one expert for grammar, one for science, and one for cooking, but trained
experts don't divide the work that neatly. When the Mixtral team checked which experts handled text
on different topics, they found no obvious pattern. What showed up instead was syntax: the word
`self` in Python code, or the spaces that indent a line, kept going to the same experts.<Cite id="jiang2024" />

</At>

## Too big to fit

<At levels="simple">

Using a quarter of the weights for each token doesn't mean storing only a quarter. The next token
might need any expert, so normally all of them sit in memory: Mixtral needs room for all 47
billion weights. When memory runs short, a program can keep the most-used experts in memory and
leave the rest on the device's storage, reading each one when the router picks it.<Cite id="eliseev2023" />
Storage holds far more but is slower to read, so every expert that isn't already in memory means a
wait.<Cite id="alizadeh2023" />

It works because the router is fairly predictable: the same experts often come up again, and a
block's router can be tried early to guess its picks before they're needed.<Cite id="eliseev2023" />
A hobby project used these tricks to run a 16-billion-weight mixture of experts on a TV box with about
2 GB of memory, at about 5 tokens a second.<Cite id="smolmaxxing" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Using a quarter of the weights for each token isn't the same as needing room for only a quarter.
The router may pick any expert for the next token, so normally every expert sits in memory: by
the rule in <ChapterRef to="quantization" />, Mixtral's 47 billion weights need about 94 GB at 16 bits and 24 GB at 4, though
each token uses 13 billion.

When memory runs short, the program running the model can keep only some of the experts in memory
and leave the rest on the device's storage, reading an expert in only when the router picks it.<Cite id="eliseev2023" />
Phones and laptops store models on flash, which holds far more than memory but is much slower to
read, so every expert that isn't already in memory is a wait.<Cite id="alizadeh2023" /> Two
habits of trained routers make this workable. The same experts often come up again for the next
token, so the program keeps the recently used ones. And since each block only adds to the token's
vector (<ChapterRef to="the-transformer-block" />), the vector going into one block is close to the one going into the next, so running
the next block's router early predicts most of its picks, and those experts can be read while the
current block works. With both, Mixtral ran on a free cloud computer with a 16 GB graphics card at
about 2 tokens a second, three times as fast as fetching each expert only when it was
needed.<Cite id="eliseev2023" />

How often the needed expert is already in memory sets the speed. A hobby project, smolmaxxing, ran
Ling-mini-2.0, a mixture of experts with 16.3 billion weights and 1.4 billion per token, on a TV
box with about 2 GB of memory. It keeps about 1.1 GB in memory: the 279 MB of weights every token uses,
and 43 of the 256 experts in each block. The other 5 GB of experts stay on flash. It writes about
5 tokens a second, and still spends about 85 of every 190 milliseconds waiting for flash.<Cite id="smolmaxxing" />
Its rule of thumb: what limits a mixture of experts on a small device is how many bytes must stay
in memory, not the total number of weights.

One more trick trades quality for speed: give the router a small bonus for experts that are
already in memory, so it picks a nearly-as-good one that needs no wait. Tried on phones, this
doubled the speed.<Cite id="skliar2025" /> In one of smolmaxxing's tests, it cut the experts that
had to be read from 24% to 14% of those picked, and made the model's perplexity (<ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />) 1.6%
worse.<Cite id="smolmaxxing" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### How much has to stay

From the shared and per-expert sizes worked out above, Mixtral at 4 bits keeps its 1.7 billion
shared weights, about 0.8 GB, in memory no matter what. Its 32 blocks each have 8 experts,<Cite id="jiang2024" />
so one expert in one block holds about $5.7 / 32 \approx 0.18$ billion weights, about 89 MB.
Each token uses 2 experts in each of the 32 blocks: 64 experts, about 5.7 GB. If none were in
memory, every token would read that much from storage, almost a quarter of the model. If 9 in 10
are found in memory, it reads about 0.57 GB. The time spent waiting is roughly

$$\text{wait per token} \approx \frac{\text{misses} \times \text{bytes per expert}}{\text{read speed}},$$

so halving the misses halves the wait, while the work done per token stays the same.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A mixture-of-experts model splits each MLP into many experts, and a router sends each token to
  only a few of them.
- The model can hold far more weights than it uses for any one token: Mixtral uses 13 of its 47
  billion.
- All the experts still have to be stored. When memory is short, most can wait on slower storage
  and be read when the router picks them; how often the needed expert is already in memory sets
  the speed.
- Experts aren't neat topic specialists; the router learns its own way of dividing the work.

<At levels="deep">

- The output is a weighted sum of the top $k$ experts; only the MLPs are split, so a token uses
  more than $k/n$ of the weights.
- At 4 bits, a Mixtral token touches 64 experts of about 89 MB each, 5.7 GB if none were in memory.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** attention keeps every earlier token around, and that gets expensive for long
texts. Could a model keep just a short summary instead?
